{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>And this for Judah, and he said: Hear, HaShem, the voice of Judah.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Sifrei 33:7) that he alluded to a blessing for Simeon in what he said: ״שְׁמַע ה׳״ and so on, for ״שְׁמַע״ is derived from the word שִׁמְעוֹן, and the matter requires explanation. And what seems closest to me to say is that they learned this from what is written ״וְזֹאת לִיהוּדָה,״ which appears superfluous, for he should have said ״וְלִיהוּדָה אָמַר,״ but rather this is its meaning: for the request ״וְאֶל עַמּוֹ תְּבִיאֶנּוּ״ was concerning Simeon, for Jacob said to him: ״וַאֲפִיצֵם בְּיִשְׂרָאֵל,״ that from him there would be teachers and paupers, and what is scattered needs gathering back to his people, for even though they were scattered among the children of Israel, nevertheless, since he is scattered, dispersed and separated from his family and from his father's house, he is not with his people. Therefore Judah prayed for him and said: Please, HaShem, bring him to his people and to his birthplace, that he not be scattered across the face of all the earth." "And it is known that for this prayer Judah too had need, as is understandable from two explanations of Rashi, who maintains that the prayer ״וְאֶל עַמּוֹ תְּבִיאֶנּוּ״ refers to Judah. And I say that Judah prayed this prayer for Simeon, and anyone who prays for his fellow and he himself needs that same thing — he is answered first (Bava Kamma 92a), and if so, that same prayer that Judah prayed for Simeon stood also for him. And this is what it says: ״וְזֹאת לִיהוּדָה״ — that prayer which he prayed for Simeon stood also for Judah. And this is what it says: ״וַיֹּאמַר שְׁמַע ה׳ קוֹל יְהוּדָה״ — that voice in which he prayed for Simeon, ״וְאֶל עַמּוֹ תְּבִיאֶנּוּ,״ meaning Judah, according to its plain sense, because in that same voice he prayed for Simeon; therefore it is just that he himself be answered first, and that he too bring him to his people. And because this prayer stood for both of them, therefore he said afterward: ״יָדָיו רָב לוֹ״ — he added the word ״לוֹ,״ for this blessing was for him alone, and Simeon had no need for the blessing of ״יָדָיו רָב לוֹ״. Only Judah, who was king, needed this blessing."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:27.254694691Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}